
  Three ways out:

  - [1mFlip the repo to ESM[0m — add [38;5;153m"type": "module"[39m to [38;5;153mpackage.json[39m, convert [38;5;153msrc/utils.js[39m ([38;5;153mmodule.exports[39m → [38;5;153mexport[39m) and
    [38;5;153msrc/index.js[39m ([38;5;153mrequire[39m → [38;5;153mimport[39m). Four lines across two files. Buys one module system repo-wide and permanently
    unblocks Node tests. Costs: it edits two files your request didn't mention, which cuts against keeping changes
    minimal — your call, not mine to take quietly.
  - [1mName it [38;5;153msettings.mjs[0m — Node treats it as ESM, [38;5;153msrc/[39m stays CommonJS, nothing unrelated is touched. Costs: the browser
    only cares about the [38;5;153mContent-Type[39m header, and some static servers (including, I believe, Python's [38;5;153mhttp.server[39m on
    some versions) don't map [38;5;153m.mjs[39m to a JavaScript MIME type, in which case the page silently fails to load the module.
    I'd want to verify that rather than assume it.
  - [1mNo unit tests[0m — verify by loading the page and checking the resolved endpoint. Zero tooling, zero unrelated edits.
    Costs: the fallback-to-production path and the hostname map go unverified, and there's no regression net when
    someone adds a fourth environment.

  [1mRecommendation: flip the repo to ESM.[0m The conversion is genuinely four lines, the two [38;5;153msrc/[39m files have no other
  consumers, and it's the only option that gets tests without a MIME-type gamble. I'm flagging the unrelated-files cost
  because it's a real tradeoff against minimal-diff discipline, not because I think it's the wrong move here.

  On the wider tooling question — this repo currently has no linter, no formatter, no test runner, and no [38;5;153mnode_modules[39m.
  [38;5;153mnode --test[39m stays zero-dependency; ESLint/Prettier would mean introducing a dependency tree and an install step to a
  repo that has deliberately avoided one.
